## Unstated is unconstrained
The spec need not be complete. An invariant states its own boundary (which inputs it covers) and
what holds there; on an input no invariant covers, any output is acceptable. A gap is never a
finding: not "this case is not covered", "no invariant decides X" or "the predicate does not say
what happens when …", nor a value a doc or invariant leaves free. A boundary that two careful
readers would apply differently, or that a program cannot check, IS a finding.

## How to answer
Walk the files in order and answer every item for each; do not stop at the first failing item.
First write `probes` for the file, then its items. For an invariant: split its predicate into
clauses (each condition of the boundary and each requirement), and for each try the edge inputs:
each way of writing what it names (quoted, escaped, attached, clustered, braced, nested inside a
substitution or expansion), one repeated or combined, one placed before, after or inside another
construct (a Function body, a subshell, a list), and one another invariant of the index also
covers. Compare parallel clauses word by word: a qualifier one clause has and its sibling lacks
("directly", "only", "exactly", "first") changes what the sibling covers. For a condition on the
characters of a text, try a text in which those characters sit inside a construct of another
kind that the condition does not name. Each probe is the input and what the text requires there; where the words allow two
answers, write both. Then answer the items from the probes.

An item fails for a reason you can quote: the words at fault. A failing item BLOCKS only when it
is material: you give a concrete input INSIDE the invariant's stated boundary, two readings of the
words at fault that careful readers could hold, and the output each reading requires
(`material`), and those outputs differ; or two invariants require different outputs on that
input. A boundary two readings apply differently is material: on that input one reading requires
an output and the other requires none (write "nothing required: outside the boundary" as its
output). Whenever you found two readings of the words at fault on a concrete input, fill
`material`; leave it out only when no reading changes what any output must be. The input of a
material answer must be one a coding agent could plausibly run in its work (`bash -lc 'npm
test'`, `cd "$HOME" || exit`, `sudo -u bob npm ci`), not a line contrived only to stress the
wording (an empty `--user=`, a function named `cd` defined after the cd, `[:digit:]` as a file
name); a contrived edge is a note. Every other failing item is a note and does not block: a missing
typed mention where the meaning is clear, a restatement or duplicate that agrees with its source,
a term re-worded from a doc, an invariant that defines terms, a field the model could add. Do not
write a material answer whose two outputs are the same, nor one for a case outside the boundary,
nor one that asks what happens where the invariant is silent.
